      <title>Record heat, melting snow: What does it mean for California’s reservoirs? • Sacramento News &amp;amp; Review</title>
      <link>https://sacramento.newsreview.com/2026/03/20/110755/?utm_source=rss&amp;utm_medium=rss&amp;utm_campaign=110755</link>
      <description>A record-baking heat wave is scalding California, with major consequences for the state’s most important reservoir: its snowpack. Providing about a third of the state’s water supply, the Sierra Nevada snowpack is a vital source of spring and summer runoff that refills reservoirs when the state needs the water most. But a warm wet storm followed February’s snow, and now, March temperatures are shattering records — prompting warnings of rapid snowmelt and swift rivers.</description>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">https://sacramento.newsreview.com/2026/03/20/110755/?utm_source=rss&amp;utm_medium=rss&amp;utm_campaign=110755</guid>
      <enclosure url="https://sacramento.newsreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/sian-bentley-magee-x9aoF0OJToU-unsplash-scaled.jpg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/>
      <pubDate>Fri, 20 Mar 2026 23:17:19 +0000</pubDate>
    </item>
